IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `eia-core`
IMPORT `er3-nouns`
IMPORT `er3-earnings`
IMPORT `er3-allocation`
IMPORT `er3-plans`
IMPORT `er3-waiting-period`
IMPORT `er3-sickness-pregnancy`
IMPORT `er3-division`
IMPORT `er3-work-sharing`
IMPORT `er3-measures-and-leaving`
IMPORT `er3-work-stoppage`
IMPORT `er3-outside-canada`
IMPORT `er3-information`
IMPORT `er3-recovery`
IMPORT `er3-seat`

-- Worked cases of the seat layer (er3-seat) for regulations 35 to 57 of the Employment Insurance Regulations
-- (SOR/96-332). Written by the piece agent er3 (Sonnet 5.5) on 2026-10-24.
--
-- Each case is a person in the seat -- someone who has lost work or cannot work, asking a question about their own
-- situation -- with the facts they could state, and an expected answer written by hand from the text of the
-- Regulations (SOR-96-332.txt lines 540-992) before the file was run. At least one case for every question. The
-- comment above the first case of a question lists the question's inputs in the order the values are given.
-- Where a case needs a date the text does not give (the day reg 39.1 or reg 56.1 came into force) it uses an
-- invented stand-in (2020-01-01) and claims nothing about it. The maximum yearly insurable earnings of reg 36(6.1)
-- is taken as 52000 so that the weekly threshold is a round 1000; it is not a published figure.
-- A case whose story says "fork Fn" asserts the encoding's own reading of an ambiguity (the fork register in
-- NOTES.md); another reading would change that case and no other.


-- Question: Is what I receive income from an employment. Inputs in order: I work under a contract of employment, whether insurable or not; I am self-employed; I hold an office as defined in the Canada Pension Plan; what I receive is money; what I receive is not money; it comes from an employer, a trustee in bankruptcy or another person.
-- Ana works under a contract of employment and is paid money by her employer: it is income from an employment (reg 35(1) "employment" (a) and "income").
#ASSERT `Is what I receive income from an employment` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- Ben has no job, no business and no office; a friend gives him money: not income from an employment.
#ASSERT `Is what I receive income from an employment` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS FALSE
-- Cleo runs her own business (self-employment, limb (b)) and is paid in goods from a customer (non-pecuniary): income from an employment.
#ASSERT `Is what I receive income from an employment` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE

-- Question: Is what I get a pension. Inputs in order: it is a retirement pension; it arises out of my employment or my service in the armed forces or a police force; it is paid under the Canada Pension Plan; it is paid under a provincial pension plan.
-- Dev gets a Canada Pension Plan retirement pension: a pension.
#ASSERT `Is what I get a pension` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Eli gets a retirement pension from a private arrangement that is not employment, armed forces, police, the CPP or a provincial plan: not a pension under reg 35(1).
#ASSERT `Is what I get a pension` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Does the income I received count as earnings. Inputs in order: purpose; it arises out of my employment; it is paid from the property of my bankrupt employer; it is a workers' compensation payment; it is a lump sum or pension in full and final settlement of a workers' compensation claim; it is a disability pension; it is paid under a sickness or disability wage-loss insurance plan; that plan is not a group plan; it is paid under a paid sick, maternity or adoption leave plan; it is paid under a leave plan for the care of my child or children; it is paid under a leave plan for the care or support of a family member; it is paid under a leave plan for the care or support of a critically ill child; it is paid under a leave plan for the care or support of a critically ill adult; it is paid by a provincial motor vehicle accident insurance plan for loss of income due to injury; the benefits under the Act are not counted by that motor vehicle plan; it is paid on account of or in place of a pension; it is a provincial indemnity for stopping work because continuing it entailed physical dangers; the benefits under the Act are not counted by that provincial law; I have since accumulated the hours required for benefits; I am self-employed and was injured before the section 152.08 period; it is paid by reason of my lay-off or separation; it is an allowance that would not be deducted under section 16(1); it is a relief grant in cash or in kind; it is a retroactive increase in wages or salary; I am a self-employed person; the pension moneys became payable before the section 152.08 period; the hours were accumulated after the pension moneys became payable; it is employment income excluded under subsection 6(16) of the Income Tax Act.
-- Fay asks, for the amount to be deducted from her benefits, whether her wages count: they arise out of her employment and nothing takes them out (reg 35(2)).
#ASSERT `Does the income I received count as earnings` `er3 purpose: the amount to be deducted from benefits` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Gus gets a workers' compensation payment every month; for an interruption of earnings under reg 14(2) it is not earnings (reg 35(4)).
#ASSERT `Does the income I received count as earnings` `er3 purpose: an interruption of earnings under subsection 14(2) of the Regulations` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
-- Gus asks the same for the deduction from his benefits: it is within reg 35(2)(b) and counts.
#ASSERT `Does the income I received count as earnings` `er3 purpose: the amount to be deducted from benefits` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Hana received a relief grant: not earnings for any purpose (reg 35(7)(c)).
#ASSERT `Does the income I received count as earnings` `er3 purpose: the amount to be deducted from benefits` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
-- Ivan was paid from the property of his bankrupt employer (reg 35(2)(a)): it counts although it does not arise out of employment.
#ASSERT `Does the income I received count as earnings` `er3 purpose: the amount to be deducted from benefits` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Jo received motor-vehicle accident insurance payments for lost income, the plan leaving out her EI benefits (35(2)(d)), and has since accumulated the hours needed: reg 35(3) takes the payments out.
#ASSERT `Does the income I received count as earnings` `er3 purpose: the amount to be deducted from benefits` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
-- Kim received lay-off pay; for an interruption of earnings under section 14 it is not earnings (reg 35(6)).
#ASSERT `Does the income I received count as earnings` `er3 purpose: an interruption of earnings under section 14 of the Regulations, other than subsection (2)` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
-- Lou receives a lump-sum pension payment on account of a pension and is self-employed, with the moneys payable before the section 152.08 period: reg 35(7)(e)(i).
#ASSERT `Does the income I received count as earnings` `er3 purpose: the amount to be deducted from benefits` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Is my wage-loss insurance plan treated as not a group plan. Inputs in order: the plan is related to a group of persons who are all employed by one employer; the plan is financed in whole or in part by an employer; I bought the plan voluntarily; my benefits would stay equivalent if I changed employer within my occupation; my premium would stay equivalent if I changed employer within my occupation; the plan provides constant benefits while permitting deductions for income from other sources; the plan's premium rates depend on the experience of that group.
-- Mia bought a private plan herself: not related to an employer's group, not financed by an employer, voluntarily purchased, portable in benefits and premium, constant benefits, premium independent of group experience: not a group plan.
#ASSERT `Is my wage-loss insurance plan treated as not a group plan` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Ned's plan is financed by his employer: a group plan.
#ASSERT `Is my wage-loss insurance plan treated as not a group plan` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
-- Ola's plan would change its premium if she changed employer: not "completely portable" under reg 35(9), so a group plan.
#ASSERT `Is my wage-loss insurance plan treated as not a group plan` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: How much of my income from my work or business counts. Inputs in order: kind; the income I received; my expenses for the direct purpose of earning it; the value of the consideration I supplied; my gross income from self-employment, without farming subsidies; my farming subsidies; my operating expenses other than capital expenditures; I receive board, living quarters or other benefits from my employer; my employer and I have fixed a value for them; the value we fixed; that value is reasonable in the circumstances; the Commission has determined a value; the value the Commission determined.
-- Pat is an employee: income 10000 less 1500 of expenses and 500 of consideration is 8000, plus 1200 of board and lodging they agreed was reasonable: 9200.
#ASSERT `How much of my income from my work or business counts` `er3 claimant who is not self-employed` 10000 1500 500 0 0 0 TRUE TRUE 1200 TRUE FALSE 0 EQUALS JUST 9200
-- Quinn is self-employed in farming: gross 20000 plus 3000 of subsidies less 5000 of operating expenses is 18000; no benefits in kind.
#ASSERT `How much of my income from my work or business counts` `er3 claimant who is self-employed in farming` 0 0 0 20000 3000 5000 FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS JUST 18000
-- Ray is self-employed outside farming: gross 15000 less 4000 is 11000; subsidies are not counted for him; no benefits in kind.
#ASSERT `How much of my income from my work or business counts` `er3 claimant who is self-employed in employment other than farming` 0 0 0 15000 999 4000 FALSE FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS JUST 11000
-- Sue is an employee with benefits in kind whose agreed value is not reasonable, and the Commission has not yet determined one: not covered.
#ASSERT `How much of my income from my work or business counts` `er3 claimant who is not self-employed` 10000 1500 500 0 0 0 TRUE TRUE 1200 FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Sue again after the Commission determines 700: 8000 plus 700.
#ASSERT `How much of my income from my work or business counts` `er3 claimant who is not self-employed` 10000 1500 500 0 0 0 TRUE FALSE 0 FALSE TRUE 700 EQUALS JUST 8700

-- Question: What is the value of my living quarters. Inputs in order: the value of the rooms or other living accommodation; the value of heat, light, telephone or other benefits included with them; the Commission has determined the value; the rental value of similar living quarters in the same vicinity or district.
-- Tom's rooms are worth 400 and heat, light and telephone 150: 550 (reg 35(13)).
#ASSERT `What is the value of my living quarters` 400 150 FALSE 0 EQUALS 550
-- The Commission valued Una's quarters by the rental of similar quarters nearby: 650 (reg 35(14)).
#ASSERT `What is the value of my living quarters` 400 150 TRUE 650 EQUALS 650

-- Question: What value is added to my income for other remuneration that is not money. Inputs in order: my remuneration is not money or only partly money; part of it is something other than living quarters and board from my employer; the value of that other thing.
-- Val is paid partly in goods worth 300 from her employer, other than board and lodging: 300 is added.
#ASSERT `What value is added to my income for other remuneration that is not money` TRUE TRUE 300 EQUALS 300
-- Wes is paid only in money: nothing is added.
#ASSERT `What value is added to my income for other remuneration that is not money` FALSE FALSE 0 EQUALS 0

-- Question: Is where I live living quarters. Inputs in order: it is rooms; it is other living accommodation.
-- Xia lives in rooms: living quarters.
#ASSERT `Is where I live living quarters` TRUE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Yan lives in a tent, which is neither rooms nor other living accommodation in his employer's sense: the inputs say neither.
#ASSERT `Is where I live living quarters` FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Is the money I received put on weeks at all. Inputs in order: it counts as earnings under regulation 35; it did not count as earnings in those weeks.
-- Zoe's pay counts as earnings and there is no sign it failed to count in those weeks: put on weeks.
#ASSERT `Is the money I received put on weeks at all` TRUE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Abe's money did not count as earnings in the weeks concerned: reg 36(2) does not put it on them.
#ASSERT `Is the money I received put on weeks at all` TRUE TRUE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: To which weeks is the money I received allocated. Inputs in order: it did not count as earnings in those weeks; it is paid under my contract of employment for the services I performed; it is paid under my contract of employment without services; it is paid to me for returning to or beginning work; I am self-employed; it is from profits or commissions; it arises from services I performed; it arises from a transaction; I am self-employed in farming; it was received as a subsidy; it is vacation pay not paid because of a lay-off or separation; it is for a specific vacation period; it is paid because of my lay-off or separation; it is paid under a labour arbitration award or tribunal judgment or a settlement; it is awarded for specific weeks after a finding that discipline was warranted; it is sick, maternity, adoption or child care leave pay; it is paid under a group sickness or disability wage-loss plan; it is a payment of a kind described in regulation 35(2)(d) or (f); it is a workers' compensation payment, not a final settlement; it is for the care or support of a family member; it is for the care or support of a critically ill child; it is for the care or support of a critically ill adult; it is pay for a holiday or non-working day; it is pay for a day next to a holiday at my employer's establishment; it is moneys on account of or in place of a pension; it is paid periodically; it is paid in a lump sum; it is a government re-employment supplement.
-- Bea is paid under her contract of employment for services she performed: to the period in which the services were performed (reg 36(4)).
#ASSERT `To which weeks is the money I received allocated` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST `er3 manner: to the period in which the services were performed`
-- Cal is paid because he was laid off: at normal weekly earnings from the week of the lay-off (reg 36(9)).
#ASSERT `To which weeks is the money I received allocated` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST `er3 manner: earnings by reason of a lay-off or separation, at normal weekly earnings from the week of the lay-off or separation`
-- Dee is paid for a public holiday: to the week the holiday occurs (reg 36(13)).
#ASSERT `To which weeks is the money I received allocated` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST `er3 manner: to the week in which the holiday or non-working day occurs`
-- Eve is self-employed and paid for a transaction: by the transaction rule of reg 36(6.1).
#ASSERT `To which weeks is the money I received allocated` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST `er3 manner: by the transaction rule of subsection (6.1)`
-- Fred is paid vacation pay for a specific vacation, not because of a lay-off: made up to normal weekly earnings (reg 36(8)(a)).
#ASSERT `To which weeks is the money I received allocated` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST `er3 manner: vacation pay for a specific vacation period, made up to normal weekly earnings`
-- Gil says none of the facts applies: no manner is given, not covered.
#ASSERT `To which weeks is the money I received allocated` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: How much of my pay for a period that is not a week is put on a given week. Inputs in order: my pay for the period; the first day of the period; the last day of the period; the Sunday that begins the week I ask about; the days I worked in that week; the days I worked in the whole period.
-- Hal is paid 1000 for 2026-03-04 to 2026-03-17 (a period that is not a week); he worked 3 of his 10 days in the week of Sunday 2026-03-01: 300.
#ASSERT `How much of my pay for a period that is not a week is put on a given week` 1000 (YMD 2026 3 4) (YMD 2026 3 17) (YMD 2026 3 1) 3 10 EQUALS JUST 300
-- Ida is paid for exactly the week 2026-03-01 to 2026-03-07: reg 36(3) does not apply (the period coincides with a week), not covered.
#ASSERT `How much of my pay for a period that is not a week is put on a given week` 1000 (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2026 3 7) (YMD 2026 3 1) 5 5 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: Where are the earnings from my transaction allocated. Inputs in order: the earnings from transactions occurring in the week; the maximum yearly insurable earnings; I did the work that gave rise to the transaction; I can show the work was done in more than one week.
-- Jon (self-employed) earned 2000 from a transaction in a week, over the weekly threshold of 1000 (52000 / 52), and did work for it: to the weeks of the work, in proportion.
#ASSERT `Where are the earnings from my transaction allocated` 2000 52000 TRUE FALSE EQUALS `er3 transaction: to the weeks in which the work was performed, in proportion to the work performed in each week`
-- Kay earned 1000, which is not greater than the threshold, and cannot show the work was done in several weeks: to the week of the transaction.
#ASSERT `Where are the earnings from my transaction allocated` 1000 52000 TRUE FALSE EQUALS `er3 transaction: to the week in which the transaction occurred`

-- Question: How much of the earnings from my transaction goes on a week. Inputs in order: the earnings from the transaction; the work I did in the week; the work I did in all the weeks.
-- Leo earned 3000 from a transaction; 2 of the 6 units of work were done in this week: 1000.
#ASSERT `How much of the earnings from my transaction goes on a week` 3000 2 6 EQUALS JUST 1000
-- No work at all is recorded in any week: no share can be worked out, not covered.
#ASSERT `How much of the earnings from my transaction goes on a week` 3000 0 0 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: How much goes on each week when my earnings are spread equally. Inputs in order: my earnings; the number of weeks falling within the period in which I earned them.
-- Mae earned 1000 over 4 weeks, neither services nor a transaction: 250 a week.
#ASSERT `How much goes on each week when my earnings are spread equally` 1000 4 EQUALS JUST 250
-- No week falls within the period: nothing to divide by, not covered.
#ASSERT `How much goes on each week when my earnings are spread equally` 1000 0 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: Which week is my farming subsidy put on. Inputs in order: the day the subsidy was paid.
-- Nan's subsidy was paid on Wednesday 2026-03-04: the week of Sunday 2026-03-01.
#ASSERT `Which week is my farming subsidy put on` (YMD 2026 3 4) EQUALS (YMD 2026 3 1)

-- Question: Which week is my pay for a holiday or non-working day put on. Inputs in order: the day of the holiday or non-working day.
-- Oz was paid for the holiday on Wednesday 2026-07-01: the week of Sunday 2026-06-28.
#ASSERT `Which week is my pay for a holiday or non-working day put on` (YMD 2026 7 1) EQUALS (YMD 2026 6 28)

-- Question: Which week are my earnings from a transaction put on. Inputs in order: the day the transaction occurs.
-- Pia's transaction occurred on Wednesday 2026-03-04: the week of Sunday 2026-03-01.
#ASSERT `Which week are my earnings from a transaction put on` (YMD 2026 3 4) EQUALS (YMD 2026 3 1)

-- Question: How much of my vacation pay for a specific vacation goes on a given week. Inputs in order: my vacation pay; my normal weekly earnings from that employment; my other earnings from that employment on each week of the vacation; the number of the week I ask about.
-- Raj has 1000 of vacation pay for a three-week vacation, normal weekly earnings 400, and 0, 100 and 0 of other earnings from the employment: week 2 gets 300.
#ASSERT `How much of my vacation pay for a specific vacation goes on a given week` 1000 400 (LIST 0, 100, 0) 2 EQUALS JUST 300
-- Raj asks about a fourth week of a three-week vacation: not covered.
#ASSERT `How much of my vacation pay for a specific vacation goes on a given week` 1000 400 (LIST 0, 100, 0) 4 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: How much of my vacation pay does not fit in the weeks of my vacation. Inputs in order: my vacation pay; my normal weekly earnings from that employment; my other earnings from that employment on each week of the vacation.
-- Sam has 1500 of vacation pay, which does not fit: 400 is left over (fork F5).
#ASSERT `How much of my vacation pay does not fit in the weeks of my vacation` 1500 400 (LIST 0, 100, 0) EQUALS 400

-- Question: How much of my vacation pay paid otherwise than for a specific vacation goes on a given week. Inputs in order: my vacation pay; my normal weekly earnings from that employment; the number of the week I ask about.
-- Tia has 1000 of vacation pay, normal weekly earnings 400: weeks of 400, 400 and 200; week 3 gets 200.
#ASSERT `How much of my vacation pay paid otherwise than for a specific vacation goes on a given week` 1000 400 3 EQUALS JUST 200
-- Tia asks about week 4: none.
#ASSERT `How much of my vacation pay paid otherwise than for a specific vacation goes on a given week` 1000 400 4 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: Over how many weeks is my lay-off or separation pay spread. Inputs in order: my pay by reason of the lay-off or separation; my normal weekly earnings from that employment; my other earnings from that employment on each week from the week of the lay-off.
-- Uma is paid 3500 on lay-off, normal weekly earnings 1000, with 0 and 200 of other earnings from the employment on the first two weeks: weeks of 1000, 800, 1000 and 700: four weeks.
#ASSERT `Over how many weeks is my lay-off or separation pay spread` 3500 1000 (LIST 0, 200) EQUALS 4

-- Question: How much of my lay-off or separation pay goes on a given week. Inputs in order: my pay by reason of the lay-off or separation; my normal weekly earnings from that employment; my other earnings from that employment on each week from the week of the lay-off; the number of the week I ask about.
-- Uma's second week gets 800 (1000 less the 200 she earned from the employer that week).
#ASSERT `How much of my lay-off or separation pay goes on a given week` 3500 1000 (LIST 0, 200) 2 EQUALS JUST 800
-- Uma's fourth week gets the remaining 700.
#ASSERT `How much of my lay-off or separation pay goes on a given week` 3500 1000 (LIST 0, 200) 4 EQUALS JUST 700
-- Uma's fifth week: the pay is used up in four weeks, nothing goes on it.
#ASSERT `How much of my lay-off or separation pay goes on a given week` 3500 1000 (LIST 0, 200) 5 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: How much of my lay-off or separation pay goes on a given week once more pay has come in. Inputs in order: the pay already allocated; the further pay; my normal weekly earnings from that employment; my other earnings from that employment on each week from the week of the lay-off; the number of the week I ask about.
-- Vic had 3500 allocated and 500 more arrives: 4000 at 1000 a week, so week 4 gets 1000.
#ASSERT `How much of my lay-off or separation pay goes on a given week once more pay has come in` 3500 500 1000 EMPTY 4 EQUALS JUST 1000
-- Vic's week 5 gets nothing: the pay is used up.
#ASSERT `How much of my lay-off or separation pay goes on a given week once more pay has come in` 3500 500 1000 EMPTY 5 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: Does the spreading of my lay-off or separation pay not apply to me. Inputs in order: the first day of my benefit period; the number of the 10 years before in which I paid at least 30% of the maximum employee's premium; the weeks of regular benefits paid to me in the 260 weeks before; the first day of the weeks my lay-off pay is allocated to; the last day of the weeks my lay-off pay is allocated to; the day I was notified of the allocation; the day I was referred to a course; the course is full-time; the course lasts this many weeks; the course costs this many dollars; my pay by reason of the lay-off or separation; I pay the whole cost of the course; the day the course begins; a ground for ending my referral exists.
-- Wyn's benefit period began 2009-06-07; he paid at least 30% of the maximum premium in 7 of the 10 years; 35 weeks of regular benefits in 260; lay-off pay of 6000 allocated 2009-06-07 to 2009-07-04, notified 2009-06-10; referred 2009-06-20 to a full-time 12-week course costing 3000, which he pays for in full and which begins 2009-09-01; no ground to end the referral: reg 36(10.1) applies, so the spreading does not.
#ASSERT `Does the spreading of my lay-off or separation pay not apply to me` (YMD 2009 6 7) 7 35 (YMD 2009 6 7) (YMD 2009 7 4) (YMD 2009 6 10) (YMD 2009 6 20) TRUE 12 3000 6000 TRUE (YMD 2009 9 1) FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Wyn again, but a ground for ending the referral exists (reg 36(10.2)): the earnings are allocated again under (9).
#ASSERT `Does the spreading of my lay-off or separation pay not apply to me` (YMD 2009 6 7) 7 35 (YMD 2009 6 7) (YMD 2009 7 4) (YMD 2009 6 10) (YMD 2009 6 20) TRUE 12 3000 6000 TRUE (YMD 2009 9 1) TRUE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: How much of my award or settlement for specific weeks goes on a given week. Inputs in order: the earnings awarded; my normal weekly earnings from that employment; my other earnings from that employment on each week from the first week awarded; the number of the week I ask about.
-- Xan was awarded 2500 for specific weeks after a finding that discipline was warranted; normal weekly earnings 1000: week 3 gets 500.
#ASSERT `How much of my award or settlement for specific weeks goes on a given week` 2500 1000 EMPTY 3 EQUALS JUST 500
-- Xan's fourth week: the award is used up in three weeks (1000, 1000, 500), nothing goes on it.
#ASSERT `How much of my award or settlement for specific weeks goes on a given week` 2500 1000 EMPTY 4 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: What weekly amount is my lump-sum pension payment treated as. Inputs in order: the lump sum I was paid; the value B calculated by the Commission.
-- Yue was paid a lump sum of 7800; the Commission's B is 78: 100 a week.
#ASSERT `What weekly amount is my lump-sum pension payment treated as` 7800 78 EQUALS JUST 100
-- A B of 0 cannot divide: not covered.
#ASSERT `What weekly amount is my lump-sum pension payment treated as` 7800 0 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: How much of my lump-sum pension payment goes on a given week. Inputs in order: the lump sum I was paid; the value B calculated by the Commission; the number of the week I ask about.
-- Zed's lump sum is 600 and B is 2.4: 250 a week; week 3 gets the last 100.
#ASSERT `How much of my lump-sum pension payment goes on a given week` 600 2.4 3 EQUALS JUST 100
-- Zed's week 4: none.
#ASSERT `How much of my lump-sum pension payment goes on a given week` 600 2.4 4 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: How much of the earnings already on a week count when my other earnings are allocated. Inputs in order: my other earnings on the week; my pension moneys put on the week under subsection (14) or (15).
-- Amy has 300 of other earnings and 200 of pension money on a week: the pension money is left out, 300 counts.
#ASSERT `How much of the earnings already on a week count when my other earnings are allocated` 300 200 EQUALS 300

-- Question: Is my employer's plan a supplemental unemployment benefit plan. Inputs in order: the plan identifies the group or groups of employees it covers; the plan covers periods of unemployment from a temporary stoppage of work, training, illness, injury or quarantine; the plan requires employees to apply for and be in receipt of benefits; the plan pays an employee who is serving the waiting period; the plan pays an employee with insufficient hours; the plan pays an employee who has received all the benefits; the plan pays an employee who is not in receipt of benefits for some other reason; the plan keeps combined weekly payments within 95 per cent of normal weekly earnings; the plan is financed by the employer with separate accounts; on termination the remaining assets revert to the employer or are used for the plan; the plan must be submitted to the Commission and changes notified within 30 days; employees have no vested right except to payments in a specified period of unemployment; guaranteed annual remuneration, deferred remuneration and severance pay are not changed by the plan.
-- Bo's plan has all nine features, paying non-recipients only while they serve the waiting period.
#ASSERT `Is my employer's plan a supplemental unemployment benefit plan` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- Cy's plan also pays some employees who are not in receipt of benefits for another reason: it fails (c).
#ASSERT `Is my employer's plan a supplemental unemployment benefit plan` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Are the payments I receive from my employer's plan left out of my earnings. Inputs in order: I receive the payments as an insured person under the plan; the plan is a supplemental unemployment benefit plan; purpose.
-- Di receives payments as an insured person under a SUB plan and asks about the deduction from her benefits: left out (reg 37(1)).
#ASSERT `Are the payments I receive from my employer's plan left out of my earnings` TRUE TRUE `er3 purpose: the amount to be deducted from benefits` EQUALS TRUE
-- Di asks for an interruption of earnings under reg 14(2): reg 37(1) does not reach it.
#ASSERT `Are the payments I receive from my employer's plan left out of my earnings` TRUE TRUE `er3 purpose: an interruption of earnings under subsection 14(2) of the Regulations` EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: What is the most my employer's plan may pay me for a week. Inputs in order: my normal weekly earnings from that employment; the portion of my weekly benefit rate from that employment.
-- Ed's normal weekly earnings are 1000 and 550 of his benefit rate comes from that employment: 95% of 1000 less 550 is 400.
#ASSERT `What is the most my employer's plan may pay me for a week` 1000 550 EQUALS 400

-- Question: How much of the top-up I receive on my leave is left out of my earnings. Inputs in order: the reason for my benefits; the payments I receive from my employer; the part of them that reduces my sick leave, vacation, severance or other credits; my normal weekly earnings from that employment; the portion of my weekly benefit rate from that employment; benefits are payable to me for that week for an illness, injury or quarantine.
-- Flo is on maternity leave: her employer pays 400, none of it reducing her credits, normal weekly earnings 1000, benefit-rate portion 550: 400 is left out.
#ASSERT `How much of the top-up I receive on my leave is left out of my earnings` `pregnancy` 400 0 1000 550 FALSE EQUALS 400
-- Flo's employer pays 600 and 250 of it reduces her credits: 350 is left out.
#ASSERT `How much of the top-up I receive on my leave is left out of my earnings` `pregnancy` 600 250 1000 550 FALSE EQUALS 350
-- In a week in which benefits are payable for illness, nothing is left out (reg 38(2)).
#ASSERT `How much of the top-up I receive on my leave is left out of my earnings` `pregnancy` 400 0 1000 550 TRUE EQUALS 0

-- Question: How much will be deducted from my benefits for earnings I had in my waiting period. Inputs in order: the earnings I had in my waiting period that count; the amounts I received in it from a wage-loss plan, workers' compensation, a 35(2)(f) indemnity or my employer's leave pay; paragraph 19(3)(a) or 152.18(3)(a) of the Act applies; the amount that paragraph requires to be deducted; my rate of weekly benefits.
-- Gus earned 500 in his waiting period and received 350 from a wage-loss plan (not counted); his weekly rate is 400: 400 is deducted (the 500 is cut to the rate).
#ASSERT `How much will be deducted from my benefits for earnings I had in my waiting period` 500 350 FALSE 0 400 EQUALS 400
-- Hui earned 500 and paragraph 19(3)(a) applies with 250 required: 250 is deducted.
#ASSERT `How much will be deducted from my benefits for earnings I had in my waiting period` 500 0 TRUE 250 400 EQUALS 250

-- Question: From how many weeks of my benefits is the deduction made. Inputs in order: I had earnings in respect of a period in my waiting period.
-- Ian had earnings in respect of a period in his waiting period: the first three weeks of benefits.
#ASSERT `From how many weeks of my benefits is the deduction made` TRUE EQUALS JUST 3
-- Jen had none: no deduction to place.
#ASSERT `From how many weeks of my benefits is the deduction made` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: Is my waiting period waived because I am an apprentice. Inputs in order: I am attending a required course of an apprenticeship program to which I was referred; I stopped working because of the course; I served a waiting period for a course of the same apprenticeship; the day I served that waiting period; the day regulation 39.1 came into force.
-- Kai attends a required apprenticeship course he was referred to, stopped working for it, and served a waiting period for the same program on 2026-02-01, after the (stand-in) day 2020-01-01: waived.
#ASSERT `Is my waiting period waived because I am an apprentice` TRUE TRUE TRUE (YMD 2026 2 1) (YMD 2020 1 1) EQUALS TRUE
-- Lin served it on the very day the section came into force: not "after" it (fork F10).
#ASSERT `Is my waiting period waived because I am an apprentice` TRUE TRUE TRUE (YMD 2020 1 1) (YMD 2020 1 1) EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Is my medical certificate enough to prove I cannot work. Inputs in order: my certificate was completed by a medical doctor or other medical professional; it attests to my inability to work; it states the probable duration of the illness, injury or quarantine.
-- Max's doctor completed the certificate, attesting to his inability to work and stating the probable duration: enough.
#ASSERT `Is my medical certificate enough to prove I cannot work` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- Nia's certificate does not state the probable duration: not enough.
#ASSERT `Is my medical certificate enough to prove I cannot work` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Can the Commission make me undergo a medical examination. Inputs in order: the Commission has directed me to undergo a medical examination; the time and place it directs are reasonable.
-- The Commission directed Ola to an examination at a reasonable time and place: yes, despite her certificate.
#ASSERT `Can the Commission make me undergo a medical examination` TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- The time and place are not reasonable: no.
#ASSERT `Can the Commission make me undergo a medical examination` TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: How much will I be reimbursed for the medical examination. Inputs in order: the Commission required the examination; my travel and other expenses of the examination; the part of those expenses that is reasonable.
-- Pru spent 120, of which 100 was reasonable, on an examination the Commission required: 100.
#ASSERT `How much will I be reimbursed for the medical examination` TRUE 120 100 EQUALS JUST 100
-- Pru went to a doctor of her own choosing: not covered by reg 40(3).
#ASSERT `How much will I be reimbursed for the medical examination` FALSE 120 100 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: Does my illness, injury or quarantine count. Inputs in order: I have an illness, injury or quarantine; it makes me incapable of doing the duties of my regular or usual employment; it makes me incapable of doing the duties of other suitable employment.
-- Quin's illness makes him incapable of his regular job and of other suitable employment: counts.
#ASSERT `Does my illness, injury or quarantine count` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- Rae's injury stops her usual job but she could do other suitable work: does not count (fork F11).
#ASSERT `Does my illness, injury or quarantine count` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Is my pregnancy that ended early treated as an illness. Inputs in order: my pregnancy was terminated; the weeks it had run when it ended.
-- Sky's pregnancy was terminated after 19 weeks: within the first 19 weeks.
#ASSERT `Is my pregnancy that ended early treated as an illness` TRUE 19 EQUALS TRUE
-- Sky's pregnancy ended at 20 weeks: not within.
#ASSERT `Is my pregnancy that ended early treated as an illness` TRUE 20 EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Could the Commission waive my waiting period because of sick leave pay. Inputs in order: I qualify for benefits by an interruption of earnings from illness, injury, quarantine, pregnancy or care; my employer or former employer pays me sick leave pay after I stopped working.
-- Tess qualifies on an interruption of earnings from illness and her employer pays her sick leave pay: the conditions are met.
#ASSERT `Could the Commission waive my waiting period because of sick leave pay` TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- No sick leave pay: not met.
#ASSERT `Could the Commission waive my waiting period because of sick leave pay` TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Have I given the Commission the proof of my pregnancy that is required. Inputs in order: I signed a statement attesting to my pregnancy; it declares my expected date of confinement; my child has been born; I told the Commission the actual date of the birth in person, by mail or by telephone; I told it as soon as was reasonably possible after the birth.
-- Uri signed a statement of her pregnancy declaring her expected date of confinement and the baby is not yet born: enough for now.
#ASSERT `Have I given the Commission the proof of my pregnancy that is required` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Uri's baby is born and she has not told the Commission the date: not complete.
#ASSERT `Have I given the Commission the proof of my pregnancy that is required` TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Does my pregnancy count. Inputs in order: I am pregnant; it makes me incapable of doing the duties of my regular or usual employment; it makes me incapable of doing the duties of other suitable employment.
-- Vera's pregnancy makes her incapable of her regular job and of other suitable work: counts.
#ASSERT `Does my pregnancy count` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE

-- Question: Can a medical certificate issued outside Canada be accepted. Inputs in order: the person who issued it is authorized abroad to do the work of a medical doctor or nurse practitioner; their qualifications are substantially similar to those of a doctor or nurse practitioner in Canada; the person the certificate is about is in that country.
-- Wade's father is ill in Portugal; a doctor authorized there, with similar qualifications, issued the certificate and the father is in Portugal: accepted.
#ASSERT `Can a medical certificate issued outside Canada be accepted` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- The patient is in Canada: not accepted.
#ASSERT `Can a medical certificate issued outside Canada be accepted` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: How many of the remaining weeks of benefits will I be paid. Inputs in order: we cannot agree on a division of the remaining weeks; a division is fixed by regulation 76.21(2) or 76.42(2); the number of remaining weeks of unpaid benefits; the number of claimants sharing them; my place in the order in which we made our claims.
-- Xi and two others cannot agree on 7 remaining weeks; Xi made the first claim: 3 weeks.
#ASSERT `How many of the remaining weeks of benefits will I be paid` TRUE FALSE 7 3 1 EQUALS JUST 3
-- The third claimant gets 2.
#ASSERT `How many of the remaining weeks of benefits will I be paid` TRUE FALSE 7 3 3 EQUALS JUST 2
-- Two weeks for three claimants: the third gets none.
#ASSERT `How many of the remaining weeks of benefits will I be paid` TRUE FALSE 2 3 3 EQUALS JUST 0
-- The claimants agreed: reg 41.6 does not apply.
#ASSERT `How many of the remaining weeks of benefits will I be paid` FALSE FALSE 7 3 1 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: Are work-sharing benefits payable to me for the week. Inputs in order: I am employed in work-sharing employment; it is a week of unemployment; the week falls in a benefit period established for me.
-- Yara is in work-sharing employment, the week is a week of unemployment in her benefit period: payable.
#ASSERT `Are work-sharing benefits payable to me for the week` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- Her week falls outside the benefit period: not payable.
#ASSERT `Are work-sharing benefits payable to me for the week` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Has an interruption of my earnings occurred because my pay was cut by at least 10 per cent. Inputs in order: my normal weekly earnings; my earnings for the week.
-- Zane's normal weekly earnings are 1000 and he earned 900: a 10 per cent cut.
#ASSERT `Has an interruption of my earnings occurred because my pay was cut by at least 10 per cent` 1000 900 EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- He earned 901: 99 is less than 10 per cent.
#ASSERT `Has an interruption of my earnings occurred because my pay was cut by at least 10 per cent` 1000 901 EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- He has no normal earnings: not covered.
#ASSERT `Has an interruption of my earnings occurred because my pay was cut by at least 10 per cent` 0 0 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: On what day does my interruption of earnings occur under work-sharing. Inputs in order: a day in the week in which my pay was cut.
-- The cut happened in the week of Wednesday 2026-03-04: it occurs on the Sunday, 2026-03-01.
#ASSERT `On what day does my interruption of earnings occur under work-sharing` (YMD 2026 3 4) EQUALS (YMD 2026 3 1)

-- Question: Am I not entitled to work-sharing benefits for the week. Inputs in order: I claim benefits under section 12 of the Act for the week.
-- Abel also claims regular benefits for the week: not entitled to work-sharing benefits for it.
#ASSERT `Am I not entitled to work-sharing benefits for the week` TRUE EQUALS TRUE

-- Question: To what period is my benefit period extended for my weeks of work-sharing. Inputs in order: the first day of my benefit period; the last day of my benefit period; my weeks of work-sharing employment in it.
-- Bram's benefit period is 2026-01-04 to 2026-07-04 and he spent three weeks in work-sharing employment: extended to 2026-07-25.
#ASSERT `To what period is my benefit period extended for my weeks of work-sharing` (YMD 2026 1 4) (YMD 2026 7 4) 3 EQUALS `Period of days` OF (YMD 2026 1 4), (YMD 2026 7 25)

-- Question: Is serving my waiting period put off because I became employed in work-sharing employment. Inputs in order: I became employed in work-sharing employment; that employment has ended; I have not served my waiting period.
-- Cora became employed in work-sharing employment before serving her waiting period: put off until it ends.
#ASSERT `Is serving my waiting period put off because I became employed in work-sharing employment` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS TRUE

-- Question: Is the deduction of my earnings put off because I became employed in work-sharing employment. Inputs in order: I became employed in work-sharing employment; that employment has ended; my earnings have not been deducted as subsection 19(1) of the Act requires.
-- Dan became employed in work-sharing employment before his earnings were deducted: put off until it ends.
#ASSERT `Is the deduction of my earnings put off because I became employed in work-sharing employment` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS TRUE

-- Question: How much will be deducted from my work-sharing benefits for the week. Inputs in order: my earnings for the week from work-sharing employment; I received other earnings for the week; the amount determined under subsection 19(2) of the Act.
-- Edna earned 300 from work-sharing employment (not deducted) and 120 other earnings, for which 19(2) gives 120: 120 is deducted.
#ASSERT `How much will be deducted from my work-sharing benefits for the week` 300 TRUE 120 EQUALS 120
-- With no other earnings, nothing is deducted.
#ASSERT `How much will be deducted from my work-sharing benefits for the week` 300 FALSE 0 EQUALS 0

-- Question: What is my weekly rate of benefits under a work-sharing agreement. Inputs in order: my rate of weekly benefits under section 14 of the Act; the hours, days or shifts I did not work because of the work-sharing agreement; the hours, days or shifts I would have worked on my usual schedule.
-- Finn's rate is 500 and he did not work 8 of his usual 40 hours: 100.
#ASSERT `What is my weekly rate of benefits under a work-sharing agreement` 500 8 40 EQUALS JUST 100
-- He has no usual schedule: not covered.
#ASSERT `What is my weekly rate of benefits under a work-sharing agreement` 500 8 0 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Text prescribes no rounding: 7 of 40 hours not worked on a rate of 500 gives the exact ratio, 87.5 (finding 4).
#ASSERT `What is my weekly rate of benefits under a work-sharing agreement` 500 7 40 EQUALS JUST 87.5

-- Question: How many of my weeks of benefits count toward the maximum. Inputs in order: the weeks of benefits paid to me; the weeks of work-sharing benefits among them.
-- Gwen has been paid 20 weeks, 5 of them work-sharing: 15 count.
#ASSERT `How many of my weeks of benefits count toward the maximum` 20 5 EQUALS 15

-- Question: Is the measure I am referred to prescribed. Inputs in order: it is an employment support measure of paragraph 59(b) of the Act; it is the subject of an agreement under section 63 of the Act; it helps participants start businesses or become self-employed; it gives participants work experience to improve their long-term prospects.
-- Hugh's measure is a paragraph 59(b) measure helping participants start businesses: prescribed.
#ASSERT `Is the measure I am referred to prescribed` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- A measure that is neither established for starting businesses nor for work experience: not prescribed.
#ASSERT `Is the measure I am referred to prescribed` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: May I be paid benefits although I left voluntarily under a work-force reduction process. Inputs in order: I left my employment in accordance with an employer work-force reduction process; my employer started the process; its objective is a permanent reduction in the number of employees; it offers employees the option to leave voluntarily; my employer documented all its elements; the process preserves the jobs of co-workers; I accepted an offer to leave voluntarily; my employer confirmed my leaving saved a co-worker's job.
-- Iris left under her employer's documented, employer-started process (permanent reduction, voluntary option) that preserves co-workers' jobs, accepted the offer, and her employer confirmed her leaving saved a job: may be paid.
#ASSERT `May I be paid benefits although I left voluntarily under a work-force reduction process` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- Her employer has not confirmed: may not.
#ASSERT `May I be paid benefits although I left voluntarily under a work-force reduction process` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Is my reason for leaving my job one of the other reasonable circumstances. Inputs in order: I must go with my partner of under a year to another residence; the first day we lived together; the day I left my job; a child was born to or adopted by one of us in that time; one of us is expecting a child; a child was placed with one of us for adoption in that time; I must care for someone; that person's relationship to me.
-- Jack left his job to go with his partner of eight months, who is expecting a child, to another residence: a listed circumstance.
#ASSERT `Is my reason for leaving my job one of the other reasonable circumstances` TRUE (YMD 2026 1 15) (YMD 2026 9 1) FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE `er3 someone else` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Kit left to care for his wife (immediate family): a listed circumstance.
#ASSERT `Is my reason for leaving my job one of the other reasonable circumstances` FALSE (YMD 2026 1 15) (YMD 2026 9 1) FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE `er3 the claimant's spouse or common-law partner` EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Lia left for a reason on neither list: not decided.
#ASSERT `Is my reason for leaving my job one of the other reasonable circumstances` FALSE (YMD 2026 1 15) (YMD 2026 9 1) FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE `er3 someone else` EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: How many days of disentitlement do I have for the week. Inputs in order: I lost a part-time job or cannot resume it because of a work stoppage under section 36(1) of the Act; an event ending the stoppage for that job has occurred; my average weekly insurable earnings in that part-time job; my weekly insurable earnings under section 14 of the Act.
-- Mo lost a part-time job to a stoppage; his part-time earnings are 500 of weekly insurable earnings of 1000, no ending event: 50% is band 2: two days.
#ASSERT `How many days of disentitlement do I have for the week` TRUE FALSE 500 1000 EQUALS JUST 2
-- More than 90 per cent (950 of 1000): five days.
#ASSERT `How many days of disentitlement do I have for the week` TRUE FALSE 950 1000 EQUALS JUST 5
-- The ending event has occurred: none.
#ASSERT `How many days of disentitlement do I have for the week` TRUE TRUE 500 1000 EQUALS JUST 0
-- He lost no part-time job to a stoppage: reg 52 does not apply, not covered.
#ASSERT `How many days of disentitlement do I have for the week` FALSE FALSE 500 1000 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: Has the work stoppage at my workplace ended. Inputs in order: the work-force level now; the part of it due to exceptional or temporary measures; the normal work-force level; the activity level now; the part of the activity due to exceptional or temporary measures; the normal activity level.
-- Nell's workplace: work-force 85 of a normal 100 and activity 90 of 100, no exceptional measures: ended.
#ASSERT `Has the work stoppage at my workplace ended` 85 0 100 90 0 100 EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- The work-force is 100 but 20 of it is due to the employer's temporary measures: 80 counts, below 85: not ended.
#ASSERT `Has the work stoppage at my workplace ended` 100 20 100 90 0 100 EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- There is no normal work-force level to compare with: not covered.
#ASSERT `Has the work stoppage at my workplace ended` 85 0 0 90 0 100 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: Has the work stoppage ended although an occurrence prevented the 85 per cent level. Inputs in order: occurrence; activity has resumed at the premises; the work-force is at 85 per cent of its adjusted normal level; the activity is at 85 per cent of its adjusted normal level.
-- After a permanent restructuring, the work-force is at 85 per cent of its adjusted normal level: ended (activity not yet).
#ASSERT `Has the work stoppage ended although an occurrence prevented the 85 per cent level` `er3 occurrence: a permanent restructuring of activity` FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- After a change in technology, activity has not resumed: not ended.
#ASSERT `Has the work stoppage ended although an occurrence prevented the 85 per cent level` `er3 occurrence: a change in technology` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Am I not disentitled by reason only of section 37 of the Act as an inmate. Inputs in order: I am self-employed; I am an inmate of a prison or similar institution; what I have been granted; it is for seeking and accepting employment in the community.
-- Ollie is an inmate on day parole to look for work: not disentitled by reason only of s 37.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled by reason only of section 37 of the Act as an inmate` FALSE TRUE `er3 release: day parole` TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- He is self-employed: reg 54(1) is not for him.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled by reason only of section 37 of the Act as an inmate` TRUE TRUE `er3 release: day parole` TRUE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Am I not disentitled by reason only of section 152.2 of the Act as a self-employed inmate. Inputs in order: I am a self-employed person; I am an inmate of a prison or similar institution; what I have been granted.
-- Pam is a self-employed inmate on a temporary absence: not disentitled by reason only of s 152.2.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled by reason only of section 152.2 of the Act as a self-employed inmate` TRUE TRUE `er3 release: a temporary absence` EQUALS TRUE
-- A certificate of availability is not on her list.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled by reason only of section 152.2 of the Act as a self-employed inmate` TRUE TRUE `er3 release: a certificate of availability` EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Am I not disentitled because I am outside Canada for a short stay. Inputs in order: I am self-employed; the reason for my stay outside Canada; the consecutive days I am outside Canada; how that person is related to me; the treatment is not readily available in the area of residence in Canada; the facility is accredited abroad; the family member is seriously ill or injured; the interview or search is bona fide.
-- Quinn goes abroad for 7 days to his mother's funeral: not disentitled for that reason.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled because I am outside Canada for a short stay` FALSE `er3 purpose: attending a funeral` 7 `er3 a father or mother of the claimant or of the claimant's spouse or common-law partner` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Eight days: too long.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled because I am outside Canada for a short stay` FALSE `er3 purpose: attending a funeral` 8 `er3 a father or mother of the claimant or of the claimant's spouse or common-law partner` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
-- A bona fide job search for 14 days: allowed; for 15 days it is not.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled because I am outside Canada for a short stay` FALSE `er3 purpose: conducting a bona fide job search` 14 `er3 someone else` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- The same for 15 days.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled because I am outside Canada for a short stay` FALSE `er3 purpose: conducting a bona fide job search` 15 `er3 someone else` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS FALSE
-- A holiday abroad for some other purpose is not on the list.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled because I am outside Canada for a short stay` FALSE `er3 purpose: some other purpose` 5 `er3 someone else` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Am I, a self-employed person, not disentitled because I am outside Canada for a short stay. Inputs in order: I am a self-employed person; the reason for my stay outside Canada; the consecutive days I am outside Canada; how that person is related to me; the treatment is not readily available in the area of residence in Canada; the facility is accredited abroad; the family member is seriously ill or injured.
-- Rosa, self-employed, visits her seriously ill child abroad for 7 days: not disentitled.
#ASSERT `Am I, a self-employed person, not disentitled because I am outside Canada for a short stay` TRUE `er3 purpose: visiting a member of the immediate family who is seriously ill or injured` 7 `er3 a child of the claimant or of the claimant's spouse or common-law partner` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- A self-employed person's job interview abroad is not on the list in reg 55.01.
#ASSERT `Am I, a self-employed person, not disentitled because I am outside Canada for a short stay` TRUE `er3 purpose: attending a bona fide job interview` 7 `er3 someone else` FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: For how many days may I be outside Canada on one trip for a funeral and a visit. Inputs in order: my trip is for a funeral and for a visit; the person I visit is the person whose funeral I attend.
-- Sid attends a funeral and visits the same person, still alive: 14 days (7 + 7).
#ASSERT `For how many days may I be outside Canada on one trip for a funeral and a visit` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST 14
-- The person visited is not the person whose funeral he attends: the periods may not be cumulated.
#ASSERT `For how many days may I be outside Canada on one trip for a funeral and a visit` TRUE FALSE EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: Is the person whose funeral I want to attend one for whom a short stay is allowed. Inputs in order: how that person was related to me.
-- Tara's grandparent died: allowed (reg 55(1)(b)(i)).
#ASSERT `Is the person whose funeral I want to attend one for whom a short stay is allowed` `er3 a grandparent of the claimant or of the claimant's spouse or common-law partner` EQUALS TRUE
-- A person who is not related in any listed way: not allowed.
#ASSERT `Is the person whose funeral I want to attend one for whom a short stay is allowed` `er3 someone else` EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Is this person a member of my immediate family. Inputs in order: how that person is related to me.
-- Uli's spouse: immediate family.
#ASSERT `Is this person a member of my immediate family` `er3 the claimant's spouse or common-law partner` EQUALS TRUE
-- Uli's grandparent: not immediate family (only a funeral may be attended).
#ASSERT `Is this person a member of my immediate family` `er3 a grandparent of the claimant or of the claimant's spouse or common-law partner` EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada while claiming special benefits or attending a course. Inputs in order: I am self-employed; the reason for my benefits; I am attending a course or program of instruction or training referred to in paragraph 25(1)(a) of the Act; my Social Insurance Number Card or its period of validity has expired.
-- Vik claims maternity benefits while abroad, SIN valid: not disentitled for that reason alone.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada while claiming special benefits or attending a course` FALSE `pregnancy` FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Her SIN card has expired: disentitled.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada while claiming special benefits or attending a course` FALSE `pregnancy` FALSE TRUE EQUALS FALSE
-- Wil attends a course under s 25(1)(a) while abroad, SIN valid: not disentitled.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada while claiming special benefits or attending a course` FALSE `a reason other than those mentioned in subsection 12(3)` TRUE FALSE EQUALS TRUE

-- Question: Am I, a self-employed person, not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada while claiming special benefits. Inputs in order: I am a self-employed person; the reason for my benefits; my Social Insurance Number Card or its period of validity has expired.
-- Xena, self-employed, claims pregnancy benefits while abroad, SIN valid: not disentitled for that reason alone.
#ASSERT `Am I, a self-employed person, not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada while claiming special benefits` TRUE `pregnancy` FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- She claims for an illness: not on the list of reg 55.01(3).
#ASSERT `Am I, a self-employed person, not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada while claiming special benefits` TRUE `a prescribed illness, injury or quarantine` FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada as a major attachment claimant who last worked abroad. Inputs in order: I am a major attachment claimant; I am a self-employed person; my most recent interruption of earnings was from insurable employment outside Canada; the reason for my benefits; I prove I am incapable of my regular or usual employment; I prove I am incapable of other suitable employment.
-- Yves, a major attachment claimant whose last interruption of earnings was from insurable work abroad, claims parental benefits: not disentitled for that reason alone.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada as a major attachment claimant who last worked abroad` TRUE FALSE TRUE `caring for a new-born or adopted child` FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Yves instead claims sickness benefits and proves he cannot do his usual work or other suitable work: not disentitled (fork F16: alternatives).
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada as a major attachment claimant who last worked abroad` TRUE FALSE TRUE `a prescribed illness, injury or quarantine` TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE

-- Question: Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I live outside Canada. Inputs in order: I am self-employed; I live outside Canada; I am a major attachment claimant under regulation 55(5); I live in a state next to Canada; I am available for work in Canada; I am able to report to an office of the Commission in Canada; I do report when the Commission asks; I am qualified under Article VI of the 1942 Agreement; I live in the District of Columbia, Puerto Rico, the Virgin Islands or a state; the Commission has not suspended the Agreement there.
-- Zoe lives in a state next to Canada, is available for work in Canada, able to report and does so when asked: not disentitled for living outside Canada.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I live outside Canada` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Zoe lives in Mexico: none of the conditions is met.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I live outside Canada` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: What is the most weeks of benefits I may be paid while I am outside Canada. Inputs in order: my benefits are for a reason mentioned in subsection 12(3) of the Act; the weeks that subsections 12(3) to (6) of the Act give for that reason; my hours of insurable employment in my qualifying period.
-- Alma, regular benefits, 1000 hours in her qualifying period: 18 weeks by the table (980 to 1,014).
#ASSERT `What is the most weeks of benefits I may be paid while I am outside Canada` FALSE 0 1000 EQUALS JUST 18
-- Alma with 1820 hours or more: 36 weeks.
#ASSERT `What is the most weeks of benefits I may be paid while I am outside Canada` FALSE 0 2000 EQUALS JUST 36
-- Fewer than 420 hours is below the table: not covered.
#ASSERT `What is the most weeks of benefits I may be paid while I am outside Canada` FALSE 0 300 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Her benefits are for a reason in s 12(3) giving 26 weeks: 26.
#ASSERT `What is the most weeks of benefits I may be paid while I am outside Canada` TRUE 26 1000 EQUALS JUST 26

-- Question: How many more weeks may I be paid now that I live in Canada again. Inputs in order: the most weeks I could be paid while outside Canada; the weeks already paid to me.
-- Bert could be paid 30 weeks and has had 12: 18 more.
#ASSERT `How many more weeks may I be paid now that I live in Canada again` 30 12 EQUALS 18

-- Question: What is the most weeks I may be paid after I move to the United States. Inputs in order: the weeks I already received in Canada; the weeks I would have had if I had lived there when my benefit period began.
-- Cat had 5 weeks in Canada; under reg 55(7) she would have had 12: 12 (the greater).
#ASSERT `What is the most weeks I may be paid after I move to the United States` 5 12 EQUALS 12

-- Question: How many weeks of different benefits may I combine while outside Canada. Inputs in order: my benefit period is extended under subsection 10(13) of the Act; the maximum weeks calculated under subsection 10(15) of the Act; the weeks I am entitled to of each kind of benefit.
-- Dov is entitled to 26, 15 and 10 weeks of three kinds of benefit: combined, at most 50.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of different benefits may I combine while outside Canada` FALSE 0 (LIST 26, 15, 10) EQUALS 50
-- His benefit period is extended (the s 10(15) maximum, invented here as 104): the cap is 102; 30 weeks entitled stay 30.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of different benefits may I combine while outside Canada` TRUE 104 (LIST 20, 10) EQUALS 30

-- Question: Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada in the course of my employment under a measure. Inputs in order: I am self-employed; the Commission approved my being outside Canada; it is in the course of my employment under a measure of paragraph 59(b) or an agreement under section 63; that measure is to help participants start businesses or become self-employed.
-- Edie is outside Canada with the Commission's approval in the course of her employment under a measure to help participants start businesses: not disentitled.
#ASSERT `Am I not disentitled for the sole reason that I am outside Canada in the course of my employment under a measure` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE

-- Question: Am I sending my claim from outside Canada in the manner required. Inputs in order: my claim is in an envelope or package addressed to the Commission; I send it by mail; I send it by certified mail; I send it by registered mail; I send it by another delivery service that provides proof of delivery.
-- Fox sends his claim by registered mail in an envelope addressed to the Commission: the manner required.
#ASSERT `Am I sending my claim from outside Canada in the manner required` TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Fox sends it by an ordinary courier that gives no proof of delivery and is not mail: not the manner required.
#ASSERT `Am I sending my claim from outside Canada in the manner required` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Will my claim sent from outside Canada be reviewed at importation. Inputs in order: my claim is in an envelope or package addressed to the Commission; I send it by mail; I send it by certified mail; I send it by registered mail; I send it by another delivery service that provides proof of delivery.
-- Gia sends her claim in a way other than reg 55(12) requires: reviewed by an employee of the Commission at importation.
#ASSERT `Will my claim sent from outside Canada be reviewed at importation` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Hal sends it by mail: not reviewed under (13).
#ASSERT `Will my claim sent from outside Canada be reviewed at importation` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: How many kinds of information will my employer tell the Commission about me each month. Inputs in order: my employer takes part in the Commission's program to substantiate claimants' proof.
-- Ina's employer takes part in the verification program: it reports commencement dates, periods of employment and amounts earned: three kinds.
#ASSERT `How many kinds of information will my employer tell the Commission about me each month` TRUE EQUALS 3
-- Jay's employer does not take part: none.
#ASSERT `How many kinds of information will my employer tell the Commission about me each month` FALSE EQUALS 0

-- Question: Could the Commission ratify the benefits I was paid while I was not entitled. Inputs in order: the day my benefit period was established; it followed a claim for special benefits; the week is after my benefit period should have expired.
-- Kes's benefit period was established 2009-06-01 after a claim for special benefits, and the week is after the benefit period that should have been established: the Commission could ratify.
#ASSERT `Could the Commission ratify the benefits I was paid while I was not entitled` (YMD 2009 6 1) TRUE TRUE EQUALS TRUE
-- A benefit period established 2011-01-01 is outside the dates.
#ASSERT `Could the Commission ratify the benefits I was paid while I was not entitled` (YMD 2011 1 1) TRUE TRUE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Could the Commission write off a penalty, an amount I owe, or the interest on it. Inputs in order: it is a penalty or an amount payable under the sections regulation 56(1) lists, or interest on one; all I owe the Crown under any program of the Department, with interest; a benefit period is running for me; I am making regular payments on a repayment plan; the debtor is deceased; the debtor is a discharged bankrupt; the debtor is an undischarged bankrupt; the final dividend has been paid; the trustee has been discharged; the overpayment arose from my error; the overpayment arose from a false or misleading declaration of mine; it arose from a retrospective decision or ruling under Part IV of the Act; it arose from a retrospective decision under Part I or IV on benefits paid under section 25; the Commission considers it uncollectable; the Commission considers that repaying it would cause me undue hardship; the Commission considers that collecting it would cost as much as it is worth.
-- Lex owes 80 in all, no benefit period running, no repayment plan: reg 56(1)(a) is met.
#ASSERT `Could the Commission write off a penalty, an amount I owe, or the interest on it` TRUE 80 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Mel owes 500; the Commission considers it uncollectable: (f) is met.
#ASSERT `Could the Commission write off a penalty, an amount I owe, or the interest on it` TRUE 500 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Ned owes 500 and none of (a) to (f) holds: not.
#ASSERT `Could the Commission write off a penalty, an amount I owe, or the interest on it` TRUE 500 FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE
-- Opal, the debtor, has died (the debtor is deceased): (b) is met.
#ASSERT `Could the Commission write off a penalty, an amount I owe, or the interest on it` TRUE 500 FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE

-- Question: How much of an overpayment for benefits received more than 12 months before I was notified could be written off. Inputs in order: the day I received the older benefit; the overpayment in respect of it; the day the Commission notified me of the overpayment; the interest accrued on that portion; the overpayment arose from my error; the overpayment arose from a false or misleading declaration of mine; a delay or error by the Commission in processing my claim; retrospective control procedures or a review by the Commission; an error on my record of employment; an incorrect calculation by my employer of my insurable earnings or hours; an error in insuring my employment or other activity.
-- Pip was paid 1000 too much in benefits received 2024-03-15 and notified 2025-06-01 (more than 12 months): not his error, caused by the Commission's delay; 50 of interest: 1050 may be written off.
#ASSERT `How much of an overpayment for benefits received more than 12 months before I was notified could be written off` (YMD 2024 3 15) 1000 (YMD 2025 6 1) 50 FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS 1050
-- If the overpayment arose from his own error: nothing under (2).
#ASSERT `How much of an overpayment for benefits received more than 12 months before I was notified could be written off` (YMD 2024 3 15) 1000 (YMD 2025 6 1) 50 TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS 0
-- Benefits received 2025-01-10 and notified 2025-06-01 are within 12 months: nothing under (2).
#ASSERT `How much of an overpayment for benefits received more than 12 months before I was notified could be written off` (YMD 2025 1 10) 500 (YMD 2025 6 1) 0 FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS 0

-- Question: Does interest run on the debt I owe. Inputs in order: it is an amount payable under section 43, 45, 46, 46.1 or 65 of the Act; a penalty was imposed on me under section 38, 39 or 65.1; a warning was issued to me under section 41.1; I was prosecuted under section 135 or 136 or the Criminal Code; the debt is an amount payable under section 38, 39 or 65.1; the debt is payable as a result of a court judgment; my penalty or warning was rescinded on appeal; I was acquitted; the debt is owing to Her Majesty under section 47 or 65.2 of the Act; the day I ask about; the day regulation 56.1 came into force.
-- Quin owes an amount payable under s 47 that is a debt of s 43-65 with a penalty imposed; owing on 2026-03-01, after the (stand-in) day 2020-01-01: interest runs.
#ASSERT `Does interest run on the debt I owe` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2020 1 1) EQUALS TRUE
-- The penalty was rescinded on appeal: no longer a debt arising from an act or omission.
#ASSERT `Does interest run on the debt I owe` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE (YMD 2026 3 1) (YMD 2020 1 1) EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: How much interest runs on my debt for a month. Inputs in order: the bank rates established during the month before; what I owe at the start of the month; the days of the month; the days of the year.
-- The Bank of Canada's rates in the month before were 4% and 5%: average 4.5%, plus 3% is 7.5%; on 36500 owed, 30 days of 365: 36500 x 0.075 x 30 / 365 = 225.
#ASSERT `How much interest runs on my debt for a month` (LIST 0.04, 0.05) 36500 30 365 EQUALS JUST 225
-- No bank rate established in that month: no average, not covered.
#ASSERT `How much interest runs on my debt for a month` EMPTY 36500 30 365 EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: On what day does the interest on my debt begin to run. Inputs in order: the day I was notified of the amount I owe.
-- Ray was notified on 2026-03-01: interest begins that day.
#ASSERT `On what day does the interest on my debt begin to run` (YMD 2026 3 1) EQUALS (YMD 2026 3 1)

-- Question: Does interest stop running on my debt while my appeal is pending. Inputs in order: an appeal or review of the decision that gave rise to my debt is pending; all that is pending is the Commission reconsidering a decision under section 41, 52 or 111.
-- Sal has an appeal of the decision pending: interest does not accrue.
#ASSERT `Does interest stop running on my debt while my appeal is pending` TRUE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Only the Commission is reconsidering a decision under s 52: that is not an appeal or review for reg 56.1(5).
#ASSERT `Does interest stop running on my debt while my appeal is pending` TRUE TRUE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: On what day does the interest on my debt stop running. Inputs in order: my repayment and the interest have been received; the day they were received; the debtor has died; the day the debtor died; the amount owed has been written off under section 56; the day the amount was written off; the accrued interest has been written off under section 56; the day the interest was written off.
-- Tom repaid on 2026-05-10 and nothing else has happened: 2026-05-10.
#ASSERT `On what day does the interest on my debt stop running` TRUE (YMD 2026 5 10) FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 5 10)
-- The debtor died on 2026-04-01, earlier than the repayment: the earliest, 2026-04-01.
#ASSERT `On what day does the interest on my debt stop running` TRUE (YMD 2026 5 10) TRUE (YMD 2026 4 1) FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2026 4 1)
-- Nothing has happened yet: no day.
#ASSERT `On what day does the interest on my debt stop running` FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) EQUALS NOTHING

-- Question: Does interest run on my debt on a given day. Inputs in order: interest runs on my kind of debt under subsection (3); the day I ask about; the day I was notified of the amount I owe; an appeal or review of the decision that gave rise to my debt is pending; all that is pending is the Commission reconsidering a decision under section 41, 52 or 111; interest has stopped running on a day; the day it stopped.
-- Uma was notified 2026-03-01; on 2026-03-10 interest runs.
#ASSERT `Does interest run on my debt on a given day` TRUE (YMD 2026 3 10) (YMD 2026 3 1) FALSE FALSE FALSE (YMD 2026 1 1) EQUALS TRUE
-- It stopped on 2026-03-10: that day is the first without interest (fork F19).
#ASSERT `Does interest run on my debt on a given day` TRUE (YMD 2026 3 10) (YMD 2026 3 1) FALSE FALSE TRUE (YMD 2026 3 10) EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Could the Commission waive or reduce the interest I owe. Inputs in order: what it would cost the Commission to collect the interest; the interest I owe; the interest is on an amount in dispute that was settled in my favour; the interest would cause me undue hardship.
-- Collecting Val's interest would cost 120, more than the 100 of interest: yes.
#ASSERT `Could the Commission waive or reduce the interest I owe` 120 100 FALSE FALSE EQUALS TRUE
-- Costs equal to the interest do not "exceed" it.
#ASSERT `Could the Commission waive or reduce the interest I owe` 100 100 FALSE FALSE EQUALS FALSE

-- Question: Can an amount be deducted from my benefits for an advance, assistance or welfare payment. Inputs in order: it is an advance, assistance or welfare payment; who paid it; the Commission has an arrangement with the payer about the deduction; the Commission has ended that arrangement; I signed the Commission's consent form.
-- Wes got welfare from a provincial government; the Commission has an arrangement with it and Wes signed the consent form: a deduction may be made.
#ASSERT `Can an amount be deducted from my benefits for an advance, assistance or welfare payment` TRUE `er3 payer: a provincial government` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
-- Wes did not sign the consent form: no deduction.
#ASSERT `Can an amount be deducted from my benefits for an advance, assistance or welfare payment` TRUE `er3 payer: a provincial government` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- The payer is a private lender, not on the list: reg 57(2) says nothing.
#ASSERT `Can an amount be deducted from my benefits for an advance, assistance or welfare payment` TRUE `er3 payer: someone else` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
